# capabilities.topic -- facilities Botlish does not have (or has only in a
# limited form), each documented as absent, with the nearest supported
# alternative. Never describe one of these as available; when the language
# or library gains one, replace its page with the real documentation.

@topic capability.overview
title: Facilities Botlish does not have, and what to use instead
summary: Every facility other languages offer that Botlish lacks or offers only in a limited form has a capability page saying so, with the nearest supported alternative; this page lists them.
index: concepts
alias: capabilities; missing features; absent features; unsupported features; does it exist; what is missing; limitations of the language
keywords: capability, absent, missing, unsupported, limitation, feature, exist, alternative
related: capability.integer-division capability.int-to-string capability.string-formatting capability.input capability.files capability.process-exit-status capability.test-timeout capability.concurrency capability.exceptions capability.multi-error-handler capability.lambdas capability.match capability.map-filter capability.null capability.assignment capability.visibility capability.struct-type-import

== HOW TO FIND OUT WHETHER SOMETHING EXISTS
Ask for it as you would name it elsewhere: `-help division`, `-help "exit
status"`, `-help "try catch"`, `-help lambda`. A facility that exists
leads to its concept or standard-library page; one that does not leads to
its capability page (the list below), whose title says "absent" or
"limited" and whose NEAREST ALTERNATIVE section says what to write. A
standard-library function that is not listed by `-help NAMESPACE` (for
example `-help str`) does not exist.

@topic capability.integer-division
title: Integer division: absent (mod exists)
summary: Botlish has no integer division: `/` divides Float64 values only, and there is no `%` or integer division function; `mod` (Euclidean remainder) exists, and bit shifts divide by powers of two.
index: concepts stdlib
alias: division; divide; integer division; quotient; div; / operator; % operator; remainder; modulo operator
keywords: divide, quotient, remainder, mod, shift_right, slash, percent
related: concept.integers concept.floats stdlib./ stdlib.mod stdlib.shift_right capability.int-to-string
docs: LANGUAGE-LIBRARY-DEFICIENCIES.md BYTE-NIBBLE-BIT-ARITHMETIC.md FLOAT64-MILESTONE-1.md
match: CORE SEMANTIC TYPE | *Botlish has no integer division*
match: SURFACE SYNTAX | unexpected character "%"*

== STATUS
`/` divides Float64 values (stdlib./) and nothing else: `7 / 2` is
rejected at compile time ("/" divides floating-point numbers, and neither
operand here is one ... Botlish has no integer division). There is no `%`
operator and no `div` function. Int is arbitrary precision, with + - *
and the comparisons.

== WHAT EXISTS
  * mod(a, b): the Euclidean remainder, 0 <= r < abs(b); mod(-7, 3) is 2.
    mod(a, 0) fails at run time (CORE SEMANTIC ARITHMETIC).
  * shift_right(n, k): n divided by 2^k, rounded down; bit_and(n, 2^k - 1)
    is the remainder (row and column of a cell of a 4x4 board, for
    example).
  * Division by any other divisor: repeated subtraction, as lib/test.bot's
    digit_value does for decimal digits (a recursive helper).
  * An approximate quotient through Float64: float64::truncate of
    float64::from_int(a) / float64::from_int(b) is exact only while both
    Ints and the quotient fit in 53 bits.

@example accept
[mod(17, 5), mod(-7, 3), shift_right(17, 2), bit_and(17, 3)]
@caption Remainder and power-of-two division
@value [2, 2, 4, 1]
@end

@example reject
x = 7 / 2
x
@caption There is no integer division
@error CORE SEMANTIC TYPE
@at 1:5
@end

@topic capability.int-to-string
title: Int-to-String conversion: absent from the standard namespaces
summary: There is no general Int-to-String conversion (no str::of, no formatting); the test framework's test::int_text(n) converts by repeated subtraction and is the one available route.
index: concepts stdlib
alias: integer to string; int to string; number to text; to_string; format number; itoa; tostring; string of a number
keywords: convert, string, text, decimal, format, int_text
related: stdlib.test::int_text concept.strings capability.string-formatting capability.integer-division
docs: LANGUAGE-LIBRARY-DEFICIENCIES.md GOLDEN-TEST-FRAMEWORK.md

== STATUS
No standard namespace converts an Int to its decimal text, and there is no
string formatting or interpolation (capability.string-formatting). A
program's value is shown by the compiler (the compiler's "value:" line, an
executable's last line), which is how most programs report numbers.

== NEAREST ALTERNATIVE
test::int_text(n), in lib/test.bot (import test), returns n in decimal with
a leading minus sign when negative. It needs no installed suite. It is
written in Botlish by repeated subtraction (there is no division), so it is
slow for huge numbers but exact.

@example accept
import test
import str

"moves: ".concat(test::int_text(20))
@value "moves: 20"
@end

@topic capability.process-exit-status
title: Setting the process exit status: absent
summary: A Botlish program cannot choose its exit status: there is no exit function; a compiled program exits 0 after printing its value, nonzero only after a run-time error.
index: concepts backends
alias: exit status; set exit code; process exit; exit function; return code
keywords: exit, status, code, process, fail, verdict
related: cli.exit-status backend.native-executable concept.program-value concept.testing

== STATUS
There is no `exit` operation and no way to make the compiler or an
executable exit with a status the program chooses. A declared error cannot
be raised to the top level deliberately either: every call's errors must be
handled or admitted, and a top-level call has no `errors` clause to admit
them in (rule.unhandled-errors).

== NEAREST ALTERNATIVE
Make the outcome the program's value and test it outside: a golden suite
returns its report (test::report(), or a verdict String such as
"GOLDEN PASS: 258 passed") and a script compares the printed value. The
compiler's own status is 1 when a program is rejected or fails at run time.

@topic capability.test-timeout
title: Per-test timeouts: absent
summary: The test framework has no timeout: a test that does not terminate hangs the suite; limit work by construction (a depth or step bound passed to the code under test) and time the whole run outside.
index: concepts
alias: test timeout; timeout; hanging test
keywords: timeout, hang, infinite loop, test, limit
related: concept.testing capability.process-exit-status
docs: GOLDEN-TEST-FRAMEWORK.md

== STATUS
lib/test.bot runs each registered check to completion, in order; Botlish
has no clock, no timer and no cancellation. A non-terminating check stops
the whole run.

== NEAREST ALTERNATIVE
Give searches and loops an explicit bound the test can choose (a depth
limit, a step count) and test the bound's behavior too; run suites under an
external timeout (the shell's `timeout`).

@topic capability.concurrency
title: Threads, parallelism and async: absent
summary: A Botlish program runs one computation on one thread: there are no threads, tasks, async/await, channels or locks; coroutines suspend and resume cooperatively, inside that one computation.
index: concepts
alias: threads; concurrency; parallelism; async; await; async await; multithreading
keywords: thread, concurrent, parallel, async, await, task, lock, channel, mutex
related: concept.coroutines concept.mutable-vector capability.test-timeout backend.overview

== STATUS
No construct or standard-library function starts a second thread of
control. A coroutine is not a thread: `step(message)` runs the coroutine
until its next `yield` while the caller waits, so at any moment exactly one
piece of Botlish code runs (concept.coroutines).

== NEAREST ALTERNATIVE
Interleave work with coroutines (a scheduler loop resuming several
handles), or run several processes from outside. The native runtime runs a
program on a worker thread of its own (for its stack guard): an
implementation detail, not something a program can use.

@topic capability.multi-error-handler
title: One handler for several errors: absent
summary: A handler names exactly one error: `on A, B:` is a syntax error. Write one handler per error, or give the callee one error with an enum payload when callers treat the failures alike.
index: concepts
alias: catch several errors; multiple errors one handler; on A, B
keywords: handler, on, several, errors, payload, enum
related: rule.one-handler-per-error task.handle-several-errors concept.errors concept.error-payloads
docs: EXPLICIT-ERROR-COMPLETIONS.md ERROR-PAYLOADS.md

== STATUS
`on A, B:` is rejected ("a handler names exactly one error: write one "on
NAME:" handler per error"). There is no catch-all handler either: every
handled error is named.

== NEAREST ALTERNATIVE
See task.handle-several-errors: one handler per error (handler values can
be the same), or, as the callee's author, one error whose payload says what
went wrong.

@topic capability.input
title: Reading input: raw bytes of standard input only
summary: There is no line or text input API; natively linux::io::read and darwin::io::read read standard input into a MutableBytes, one read(2) at a time, through the platform's I/O context.
index: concepts stdlib
alias: read input; stdin; read line; user input; keyboard input; standard input
keywords: input, read, stdin, line, console, bytes
related: concept.argv backend.io capability.files stdlib.linux::io::read stdlib.module.linux::io stdlib.abi::bytes::MutableBytes
docs: CONTEXT-TRAITS.md

== STATUS
lib/io.bot is output only (print, print_line, write_error,
write_error_line). Standard input is read through the Linux context
itself, as bytes:

    import abi
    import abi::bytes
    import linux::io

    fn first_bytes() -> int:
        r = linux::io::read(abi::bytes::zeroed(abi::usize(16)))
        r.result                    # the byte count (or -errno)

    with context linux::io::create()
    first_bytes()

r.data is the buffer with the bytes read. On macOS the same is
darwin::io::read through `with context darwin::io::create()`: its
result holds the count (0 at end of file, -1 on failure) and the errno
of a failure as separate fields, libSystem's convention. The call needs
no direct module
(the context mediates it), but it runs only on cranelift,
cranelift-generic and native executables: interp and compile refuse it
(NATIVE-ONLY). There is no line splitting or end-of-file helper (read
until the result is 0), and no UTF-8 decoding of bytes into a String in
the standard namespaces: what is read stays bytes.

== NEAREST ALTERNATIVE
For small, fixed inputs pass them in the program text or through argv()
(concept.argv), which works on every backend.

@topic capability.files
title: Files and directories: absent from the portable library
summary: There is no portable file or directory API; the path modules handle path text, and only macOS's low-level darwin module opens files (open, create, close through libSystem).
index: concepts stdlib
alias: open file; read file; write file; file system; filesystem
keywords: file, open, read, write, close, directory, path, darwin
related: capability.input backend.io stdlib.module.io::path stdlib.module.linux stdlib.module.darwin
docs: MACOS-AARCH64-MILESTONE-8.md

== STATUS
No portable open/read/write of named files. io::path, linux::path and
darwin::path are textual path utilities. On macOS, lib/darwin.bot's
low-level operations open (darwin::open), create (darwin::create,
darwin::create_new: open(2)'s variadic creation mode), read, write and
close a descriptor through libSystem, each one C call returning its count
or descriptor and the errno of a failure; they are a direct module's raw
authority, run on the native backends only, and own nothing (closing is
the caller's). On Linux, lib/linux.bot's raw read and write system calls
are the only route, and no open exists.

@topic capability.string-formatting
title: String formatting and interpolation: absent
summary: There is no format function, template string or interpolation; text is built with str::concat and str::append_char, numbers with test::int_text.
index: concepts stdlib
alias: string formatting; format string; interpolation; sprintf; string interpolation; f-string
keywords: format, interpolation, template, concat, text
related: task.string-concatenation capability.int-to-string concept.strings stdlib.str::concat

== STATUS
No format, printf-style operation or interpolation syntax exists.

== NEAREST ALTERNATIVE
Concatenate: `"moves: ".concat(test::int_text(n)).concat(" steps")`; build
long texts with a helper or lib/test.bot's test::joined(parts, separator).

@topic capability.assignment
title: Assignment and mutable variables: absent
summary: Bindings are immutable and a name is bound once per scope: `x = x + 1` is a duplicate binding; compute a new name, recurse, collect with a loop, or grow a MutableVector.
index: concepts
alias: assignment; mutable variable; reassign; variable update; counter variable; x = x + 1
keywords: assign, mutate, variable, counter, accumulate, update
related: concept.bindings rule.immutable-bindings principle.immutability concept.loops concept.mutable-vector

== STATUS
`x = 1` then `x = x + 1` in the same scope is DUPLICATE. A nested scope may
shadow a name with a new binding, which never changes the outer one.

== NEAREST ALTERNATIVES
  * a new name: `next = x + 1`;
  * a collecting loop for per-element values: `squares = loop x in xs: x *
    x`;
  * recursion, carrying the accumulator as a parameter;
  * a MutableVector (a mutable value with checked places) when growing a
    collection in place is the point.

@example accept
import list

fn sum(xs: List[int], i: int, total: int) -> int:
    if i < 0 or i >= xs.length():
        return total
    xs.sum(i + 1, total + xs.at(i))

[1, 2, 3].sum(0, 0)
@caption An accumulator carried as a parameter
@value 6
@end

@topic capability.null
title: null, nil and optional values: absent
summary: Botlish has no null, no nil and no optional type: a function that may not have a result fails with a declared error, or takes a default from its caller.
index: concepts
alias: null; nil; none; optional; maybe; undefined
keywords: null, optional, missing, absent, default
related: principle.errors-over-sentinels concept.errors stdlib.list::get capability.match
match: CORE SEMANTIC UNBOUND | unbound name "null"*

== STATUS
`null` is just an unbound name. No value means "nothing" except unit, which
is a value of its own type, not a member of every type.

== NEAREST ALTERNATIVE
Declare an error (`fn find(...) errors NotFound`) and handle it at the call
(principle.errors-over-sentinels); or take a default argument as
list::get(xs, i, default) does.

@example reject
x = null
x
@error CORE SEMANTIC UNBOUND
@at 1:5
@end

@topic capability.visibility
title: Private module members: absent
summary: A module has no visibility control: every top-level function and value of a module is reachable after `import`; representation privacy exists for structs only, through `opaque struct`.
index: concepts
alias: private; public; visibility; export; hide function
keywords: private, public, export, module, opaque
related: concept.opaque-structs concept.modules rule.opaque-authority

== STATUS
No `private`, `pub` or export list. Helpers of a module are importable like
its API.

== NEAREST ALTERNATIVE
`opaque struct` makes a struct's fields and construction belong to its
module: everywhere else the value can be passed and compared but not built
or inspected, so invariants hold by construction.

@topic capability.match
title: switch, match and pattern matching: absent
summary: There is no switch or match statement: branch with if/elif/else (conditions in order), compare enum cases with ==, and destructure structs by field.
index: concepts
alias: switch; match; case statement; pattern matching
keywords: switch, match, case, pattern, enum, elif
related: concept.if-expressions concept.enums concept.destructuring capability.null

== STATUS
No switch/match/case construct and no patterns beyond struct
destructuring (which binds fields; it never tests values).

== NEAREST ALTERNATIVE
    if d == Direction::Up:
        ...
    elif d == Direction::Down:
        ...
    else:
        ...
The chain is an expression too (`name = if ...`).

@topic capability.lambdas
title: Anonymous functions (lambdas): absent
summary: There is no anonymous function expression: declare a nested named function, which captures its environment and is a value you can pass.
index: concepts
alias: lambda; lambdas; anonymous function; closure expression; arrow function
keywords: lambda, anonymous, closure, callback, fn expression
related: concept.closures concept.functions concept.function-types
match: SURFACE SYNTAX | expected an expression, found "fn"*

== STATUS
`fn` starts a declaration only; `g = fn(y): y` is a syntax error
(expected an expression, found "fn").

== NEAREST ALTERNATIVE
    fn big?(x: int) -> bool:
        x > 10
    xs.any?(big?)
A nested function sees the bindings around it (concept.closures).

@example reject
g = fn(y): y
1
@error SURFACE SYNTAX
@at 1:5
@end

@example accept
import list

fn has_big?(xs: List[int], limit: int) -> bool:
    fn big?(x: int) -> bool:
        x > limit
    xs.any?(big?)

[3, 12].has_big?(10)
@caption A nested function capturing `limit`
@value true
@end

@topic capability.map-filter
title: map, filter and reduce: use loops
summary: There are no map/filter/reduce functions: a collecting loop is the transformation (`loop x in xs: f(x)`), `continue` filters, and list::any?, all?, none? and find cover search.
index: concepts stdlib
alias: map; filter; reduce; fold; comprehension; list comprehension
keywords: map, filter, reduce, fold, transform, collect
related: concept.loops stdlib.module.list capability.assignment
docs: RETURNING-ITERABLE-LOOPS.md COLLECTING-LOOPS.md

== STATUS
lib/list.bot deliberately has no map or filter: the collecting loop
subsumes them. There is no fold either; carry an accumulator through
recursion, or use a MutableVector.

@example accept
evens = loop x in [1, 2, 3, 4]:
    if x.mod(2) == 1:
        continue
    x * 10
evens
@caption map and filter as one loop
@value [20, 40]
@end

@topic capability.exceptions
title: Exceptions (try, catch, throw): declared errors instead
summary: No exceptions: a failure is a declared error, raised with `fail`, listed in the function's `errors` clause, handled by name with `on NAME:`; no catch-all, no finally, no unchecked throw.
index: concepts
alias: try catch; try/catch; throw; raise; exception; exceptions; catch; finally
keywords: exception, throw, catch, try, raise, error, handler
related: concept.errors concept.error-payloads rule.unhandled-errors rule.one-handler-per-error concept.known-errors capability.multi-error-handler
docs: EXPLICIT-ERROR-COMPLETIONS.md

== HOW THE CONCEPTS MAP
    throw / raise E      fail E            (E a declared error: `error E`)
    throws E (Java)      errors E          (in the function's signature)
    try { f() }          f():              (the call, then its handlers)
    catch (E e) { ... }      on E:         (one handler per error; payload
                                            fields with `on E {field}:`)
    catch-all, finally   (none)
Differences that matter: errors are checked statically -- every call must
handle, admit or be proven unable to raise each error it may raise -- and a
call proven to always fail is rejected (concept.known-errors). A handler's
value is the call's value, or it leaves with return/fail.

@example accept
import list

error Empty

fn first(xs: List[int]) -> int errors Empty:
    if xs.length() == 0:
        fail Empty
    xs.at(0)

fn safe_first(xs: List[int]) -> int:
    first(xs):
        on Empty:
            -1

safe_first([])
@value -1
@end

@topic capability.struct-type-import
title: Importing a struct's short name: absent
summary: `import type NS::T` imports type declarations and enums, not structs: a struct of another module is always spelled NS::Struct after `import NS`.
index: concepts
alias: import struct; import struct type; import type struct
keywords: import type, struct, qualified, short name
related: rule.import-type concept.imports concept.structs concept.enums
docs: IMPORTS.md

== STATUS
`import type puzzle15::Board` for a struct is rejected (NOT-A-TYPE: "is a
struct, not a source-defined `type`"); enums and `type` declarations are
importable by short name.

== NEAREST ALTERNATIVE
Spell the struct qualified (`puzzle15::Board`) after `import puzzle15`.
